I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided PXRD/GSAS-II tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them in a single multiphase project, and validate the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and statistics against that final project.

The chemistry points first to ZnWO₄ with possible ZnO/WO₃ residue, but I’ll treat that only as a candidate set. I’m now checking the frozen reference index and the pattern’s peak families so phase count is driven by indexed reflections, not by the reaction label.

Peak positions confirm a wolframite-type ZnWO₄ majority phase. A separate narrow family begins at about 23.41°, 26.99°, 28.91/29.24°, and 33.60°, which is consistent with residual monoclinic WO₃; several ZnO reflections may overlap the majority phase. I’m moving to staged whole-pattern refinements to test whether ZnO has a stable nonzero scale and to discriminate the WO₃ polymorphs.

The three-phase trial gives stable nonzero scales for both residuals—roughly 85% ZnWO₄, 11% WO₃, and 4% ZnO before final model refinement. I’m comparing monoclinic and triclinic WO₃ references at matched refinement depth; the current fit is still deliberately provisional because unconstrained profile parameters exposed strong correlations.

The monoclinic WO₃ model is preferred: the triclinic trial relaxes back toward monoclinic metric symmetry and fits slightly worse. Refining the two heavy-atom coordinates in transferred ZnWO₄ improves the fit and gives plausible shifts; unconstrained oxygen refinement moves too far, so I’m rolling that back and checking cleaner isostructural coordinate starts before accepting any oxygen positions.

The physically clean analogue starts do not outperform the CoMoO₄-II transfer at matched depth; the first alternative is about 2.3 Rwp points worse. I’m completing the remaining checks, then I’ll retain the best start and add only bounded, chemistry-consistent coordinate/profile freedom.

A [111] March–Dollase texture term is decisively supported: it lowers Rwp from about 17.1% to 11.1%, while the other four allowed axes change Rwp by less than 0.04 point. The refined ratio is about 0.70, exactly the kind of phase-specific texture permitted by the measurement model, and it resolves the diagnostic 31° intensity doublet without inventing another phase.

The dominant phase also prefers anisotropic broadening. An unconstrained [010] model overreaches beyond the generator’s allowed strain ratio, so I’m testing the allowed boundary explicitly rather than accepting that overfit; [100] already gives a smaller, fully physical improvement.

The first bounded oxygen trial appears to have triggered GSAS-II’s limit-freezing behavior. I’m replaying every candidate with zero refinement cycles now; this is essential because a post-limit stored profile can otherwise lag the clamped structural parameters. Only gains that survive that fresh replay will be retained.

After physical ADP normalization, the replay-stable fit is Rwp ≈10.24% with about 81% ZnWO₄, 12.5% WO₃, and 6.6% ZnO. The fraction shift is expected because the original WO₃/ZnO references carried zero or anisotropic published ADPs, whereas the synthetic measurement uses one isotropic value per phase. I’m now refining only the two heavy W sites in WO₃ before considering any light-atom motion.

The phase set has converged to wolframite ZnWO₄ with minor monoclinic WO₃ and wurtzite ZnO. I’m now doing the final full-grid replay, checking that residual peaks belong to those accepted reflection families, and then I’ll export the project, CIFs, fractions, profile, and concise uncertainty report.

The final replay confirms the dominant phase’s heavy-atom coordinates can be improved slightly without hitting bounds or creating bad geometry; that gain survives a fresh calculation. I’m retaining that small update and testing only the remaining symmetry-allowed ZnO internal coordinate before freezing the model.

All prominent minor-phase lines are now accounted for by the three accepted structures. The ZnO oxygen parameter is only weakly constrained; its profile optimum lies beyond the allowed reference-perturbation range, so I have fixed it at the last physically admissible value rather than extrapolating. The resulting tetrahedral Zn–O distances remain normal, but I’ll flag that coordinate as uncertain in the report.

The final three-phase model is stable on a complete 9.903–100.095° replay with no frozen or out-of-bounds variables. I retained the conservative oxygen refinement rather than the lower-Rwp but increasingly distorted alternatives; the current fresh-replay statistics are Rwp ≈10.08% and Rp ≈5.95%. I’m exporting and independently validating the exact saved project now.

